1
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:07,280
On the night of August 15th, 1977, Ohio State's

2
00:00:07,580 --> 00:00:10,760
Big Ear radio telescope was listening to the sky.

3
00:00:11,780 --> 00:00:16,119
For 72 seconds, it heard something, a signal so

4
00:00:16,259 --> 00:00:20,480
strong and so narrow that days later, the astronomer

5
00:00:20,660 --> 00:00:23,920
reading the printout circled it in red pen and

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:25,660
wrote one word next to it:

7
00:00:25,680 --> 00:00:26,980
"Wow!"

8
00:00:29,060 --> 00:00:30,340
It was never heard again.

9
00:00:35,980 --> 00:00:38,420
The search had begun 17 years earlier.

10
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:43,599
In 1960, in West Virginia, a young astronomer named

11
00:00:43,720 --> 00:00:47,180
Frank Drake pointed a radio dish at two nearby

12
00:00:47,279 --> 00:00:50,300
stars, much like our Sun, and listened.

13
00:00:51,459 --> 00:00:54,879
He tuned to the natural frequency of hydrogen, the

14
00:00:54,940 --> 00:00:57,059
most common element in the universe.

15
00:00:57,980 --> 00:01:00,599
If anyone out there wanted to be heard, the

16
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:03,500
reasoning went, that's where they might call.

17
00:01:04,860 --> 00:01:07,400
For four months, he heard nothing but static.

18
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:12,759
Ten years before that, the physicist Enrico Fermi had

19
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:14,700
asked a simple question over lunch:

20
00:01:14,720 --> 00:01:18,259
"If the galaxy is so old and so vast,

21
00:01:18,880 --> 00:01:19,759
where is everybody?"

22
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:24,740
Since then, we've listened with bigger dishes and better

23
00:01:24,820 --> 00:01:25,460
computers.

24
00:01:26,420 --> 00:01:30,759
In 2019, astronomers even caught a signal that seemed

25
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:32,899
to come from our nearest neighboring star.

26
00:01:34,039 --> 00:01:36,259
It turned out to be our own electronics.

27
00:01:37,380 --> 00:01:40,399
More than 60 years later, the answer is still

28
00:01:41,119 --> 00:01:41,640
silence.

29
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:45,500
But what if we've been listening for the wrong

30
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:45,880
thing?

31
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:49,179
Look at Earth from far away.

32
00:01:50,140 --> 00:01:53,539
We aren't deliberately sending the universe a message, but

33
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:54,539
we are changing it.

34
00:01:55,520 --> 00:01:56,920
Our cities glow at night.

35
00:01:57,580 --> 00:01:59,759
Our machines leak radio into space.

36
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:03,619
We've even changed the chemistry of our own air.

37
00:02:04,780 --> 00:02:07,539
So scientists now search for something else:

38
00:02:08,460 --> 00:02:09,539
technosignatures.

39
00:02:11,340 --> 00:02:16,020
Not a hello, just evidence that someone, somewhere, built

40
00:02:16,220 --> 00:02:16,638
something.

41
00:02:17,820 --> 00:02:21,340
Artificial light on a distant planet, a gas in

42
00:02:21,359 --> 00:02:25,540
its air that nature doesn't make, heat from machines.

43
00:02:26,780 --> 00:02:30,040
Not a voice calling to us, just the traces

44
00:02:30,260 --> 00:02:33,760
a civilization leaves behind, the way ours does.

45
00:02:35,380 --> 00:02:38,180
And then there's the question closer to home.

46
00:02:39,260 --> 00:02:42,440
For years, military pilots have reported things in the

47
00:02:42,460 --> 00:02:44,200
sky they couldn't identify.

48
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:47,680
In 2021, a U.S.

49
00:02:47,799 --> 00:02:52,280
intelligence report reviewed 144 of those reports.

50
00:02:53,420 --> 00:02:55,940
It could confidently explain only one:

51
00:02:56,880 --> 00:02:58,700
a large deflating balloon.

52
00:02:59,920 --> 00:03:02,160
Later reviews explained many more.

53
00:03:03,359 --> 00:03:07,540
And in 2024, a Pentagon office reviewing decades of

54
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:11,299
records found no evidence of alien technology.

55
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,140
Unexplained doesn't mean alien.

56
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:17,640
It means unexplained.

57
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:20,500
So have we really looked?

58
00:03:21,940 --> 00:03:26,600
In 2018, astronomers estimated how much of the possible

59
00:03:26,739 --> 00:03:29,480
search space all our efforts had covered.

60
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:34,320
Compare it to all of Earth's oceans and everything

61
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:37,320
we've ever done is about one hot tub of

62
00:03:37,400 --> 00:03:37,720
water.

63
00:03:39,260 --> 00:03:41,540
If you scooped a single hot tub out of

64
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:45,420
the ocean and found no fish, you wouldn't conclude

65
00:03:45,620 --> 00:03:46,739
the ocean is empty.

66
00:03:47,960 --> 00:03:51,859
So no, there is no confirmed evidence that aliens

67
00:03:51,900 --> 00:03:52,340
are here.

68
00:03:53,500 --> 00:03:56,100
But maybe the silence isn't an answer.

69
00:03:57,299 --> 00:04:00,320
Maybe we just haven't learned how to listen
